    {
      "id": "claim_5712564d44244197a155ef4bdc054f9f",
      "statement": "Each picked file gets a fresh AES-256-GCM key that is exported raw and RSA-wrapped with the receiver's public key — and a pick made while encryption is on but the peer's rsaPub has not been imported yet (the offer is still pending) is rejected with an error toast instead of being registered with an empty wrapped key. The wrapped key bytes travel in MetaData.key and the receiver unwraps them with its private key in onMetaData, keeping the result on the file detail for chunk decryption; before that unwrap, onMetaData sanitizes the peer-controlled MetaData.name via sanitizeName (one rule covering all sinks — the bulk store-only ZIP archive and <a download> — so a path-traversal name can no longer reach either, PR #98). The receiver registers the file entry at WaitingAccept BEFORE the async key decryption, and on unwrap failure sets a visible error on the entry, toasts, sends EVENT_VALIDATE_ERROR to the sender, and returns without accepting. A metadata-failed entry (error set, or missing aesKey on an encrypted session) additionally cannot be force-accepted: Receiver.onAccept guards its entry point and refuses such entries with a toast plus EVENT_RECEIVER_REJECT instead of starting a transfer that can never succeed (PR #78). Pick-time crypto failures are equally visible: onFilesPick iterates with an async for...of (not a fire-and-forget forEach) so a generateAesKey/encryptAesKeyWithRsaPublicKey rejection toasts and skips the entry instead of escaping as an unhandled rejection (PR #75). A metadata-validation failure at pick time is treated the same way (PR #96): the shared validateFileMetadata rejects size <= 0 / non-finite (e.g. a 0-byte file), toasts, and continues — the entry is never registered in sendingFiles, because such a file can never complete (the receiver bounces it with EVENT_VALIDATE_ERROR on every resend and processChunk's exact-match gate can never be satisfied by an empty payload), so registering it as a Resend-able Pending entry was a guaranteed dead-end that re-fired a 'Receiver validate error' toast on every retry.",
